WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770
You just saw how Multi-vector approaches can be used in text retrieval.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.072
Now, let's build on what you learned

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:07.674
and look at how a similar

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.476
approach can be used in Image Retrieval.

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:11.878
All right, let's go. Image retrieval is

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.947
important because a lot of useful information

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:16.850
is not stored as text. It's stored in image files.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:19.319
Sometimes this means actual images,

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:22.822
but scan documents, PDF files or slide decks

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.824
are also essentially images.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.794
A new generation of vision language models

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:30.196
are able to accept both text and images as input.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.799
So if you retrieve the right documents,

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:35.001
you can build capable multimodal RAG systems.

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:37.670
The challenge is completing that retrieval step.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:41.674
Documents are the most common type of image companies want to retrieve.

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:44.277
But they present a lot of challenges for retrieval.

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:48.081
Slides or document pages can be a complex mix of text,

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:51.084
images, charts, and tables.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:54.788
The traditional approach for parsing this kind of document

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:56.256
is to first use special models

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:57.957
to detect the layout of the document

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:01.061
and then parse each content type separately.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:04.764
This approach requires you to build a complicated custom system

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:06.766
that often is still fallible.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:10.070
What's worse, a retrieval pipeline for one kind of document

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.372
often won't work on another.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:18.144
ColPali, a multi-vector image retrieval approach, addresses many of those issues.

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:23.049
ColPali stands for Contextualized Late Interaction Over PaliGemma.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:26.553
PaliGemma is the name of a vision language model created by Google.

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:30.156
However, the name ColPali is also quite often used to describe

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:33.159
the whole family of models built on the same principles.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:37.163
Multi-vector embedding models built on top of vision language models,

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:39.566
which can take both images and text as input.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:43.503
This is similar to ColBERT, which is a specific model,

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:45.839
but also is often used to refer to

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:48.141
a whole family of multi-vector text retrieval techniques.

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:50.143
ColPali has many great features.

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:53.780
It skips the complicated and error-prone format detection step,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.749
and it works flexibly across all document types.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.819
While it can be somewhat costly to run,

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:02.188
it can also end up being cheaper in the long run

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:04.991
because your system is relying on a single model

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:08.495
instead of many models that traditional approach relied on.

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:11.798
Let's see how vision language models are adapted for retrieval

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.800
and then apply them in practice.

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:17.637
A typical workflow over a large language model goes as follows.

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:20.640
Your input text is passed to a Tokenizer.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.843
The role of the Tokenizer is to split it into smaller units

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:27.347
called tokens and to create a sequence of token IDs

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.549
which is then passed to a model.

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:34.687
In reality, the model translates these IDs into their corresponding token embeddings

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.289
it learned during the training.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:40.293
Those vectors are static and specific to each of the tokens.

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:42.996
For the time being, let's assume that the input

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:45.598
of the language model is a sequence of token embeddings.

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.901
The language model passes the sequence

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:49.903
through its own layers and produces

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:52.205
a contextualized embedding of the current token

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:54.908
that takes the whole sequence into consideration.

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:57.510
This vector is typically used to generate logits

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.712
and then probabilities of each of the tokens

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:01.514
in a vocabulary

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.416
to be a continuation of the sequence.

00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:05.718
And Vision Language Models are not different here,

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.921
if we use them for text generation.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:10.456
However, adding another modality

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:13.359
requires some extensions of that architecture.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.129
Vision is an entirely different modality,

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:18.331
so it cannot be converted into text directly.

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:21.434
Thus, it has its own preprocessing pipeline,

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:23.736
which obviously starts with an entire image.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.906
The overall goal is to process the image in such a way

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:29.609
that you input it as a sequence of embeddings

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:32.312
into the language model, like you do with text.

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:35.315
So the image is being processed through an additional transformer

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:37.917
which actually converts it into image patches

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:40.320
of a specific size as a first step.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:44.591
A single image becomes a sequence of patches, not a single input.

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:47.527
Similar to text, which is processed as a

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:50.063
sequence of tokens, not as an entire document.

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:51.998
Image input is passed through a

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:53.967
neural network that is a Vision Transformer,

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:58.171
and it again produces a sequence of contextualized vector embeddings,

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.473
one for each input patch,

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:02.575
typically highly dimensional.

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:06.779
The output has to be then projected onto a smaller dimensional space

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.481
and then passed to the language model.

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:11.718
The reason for the last step is to make the image vectors

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:14.721
have the same number of dimensions as the text vectors.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:18.224
All in all, this additional process enables a different modality

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:20.326
to be passed to the same network.

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:24.130
No matter the input modality, the language model will produce an embedding

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.399
that will be used to generate logits

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:28.501
and probabilities of each of the tokens

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:30.603
later on in the generation process.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.040
So ultimately, a typical vision language model

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.943
adds some extra processing to the images,

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:41.047
while keeping the text processing intact compared to pure language model.

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:45.051
A VLM still predicts the next token given the input,

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:47.954
so we are not done yet with the embeddings generation.

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.923
You don't want a generative system,

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.426
but something that will represent an input

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:55.628
for the retrieval in a form of vector embeddings.

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:58.231
In order to produce vector embeddings,

00:04:58.231 --> 00:05:00.633
you need to slightly extend the logic of the VLM

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:02.902
and add another projection layer

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:05.204
just right after the language model generates

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:07.206
the hidden states from its final layer.

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:11.144
This projection layer reduces embeddings for efficient storage.

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:15.815
For Gemma, it goes from 2048 to 128 dimensions,

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:17.884
but it may differ between the models.

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:20.753
There is no pooling at the very end,

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:24.324
so ultimately, the entire input sequence, no matter the modality,

00:05:24.524 --> 00:05:26.526
is transformed through a dense network

00:05:26.526 --> 00:05:28.194
and you get a sequence of

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:30.096
the same length that represents your input.

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:32.498
In general, it's not enough

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:35.401
to just start using VLMs for vector retrieval.

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:38.204
There is some additional training required

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:40.406
for both the final projection layer

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:42.408
and language model parameters.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.478
Many papers suggest using low rank adapters

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:47.680
to the language model attention weights.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.385
Low rank adaptation adds low rank matrices to the frozen base model.

00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:55.855
The term low rank refers to the dimensional complexity,

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:59.459
where a lower rank means the matrix can be represented

00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:01.661
as the product of two smaller matrices.

00:06:02.195 --> 00:06:04.297
For example, instead of training

00:06:04.297 --> 00:06:08.000
a full 2048 by 2048 weight matrix,

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:10.803
requiring over 4 million parameters.

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:13.406
LoRA uses two matrices of size

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:17.377
2048 by 32 and 32 by 2048,

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:21.381
requiring only around 131000 parameters,

00:06:21.581 --> 00:06:23.983
which is a 97% reduction.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.321
The training process adjusts the whole model for effective retrieval,

00:06:28.421 --> 00:06:32.625
but due to these optimizations, it's easier and faster than full fine-tuning.

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:34.961
Practically, you don't need to know

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:37.330
all these details to start using ColPali.

00:06:37.497 --> 00:06:41.300
You will start by loading the appropriate model based on available hardware.

00:06:41.501 --> 00:06:43.703
While ColPali is a more powerful one,

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:45.371
it requires a lot of memory

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:47.306
to run or even better a GPU.

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:50.009
For that reason, you can also use ColSmol,

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:54.714
which is a smaller variant with 256 million parameters

00:06:54.714 --> 00:06:58.017
that you are able to use here on the deep learning platform.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:01.487
Feel free to run it on a different environment

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:04.390
with the full ColPali model, so you can see the difference.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:07.193
No matter which model you end up using,

00:07:07.226 --> 00:07:09.395
you need a processor that works with raw data

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:12.698
and converts it into a representation that the model can handle.

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:14.567
Let's load a page from a

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:16.202
famous Attention is all you need paper

00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:19.572
to see how ColPali processes visual documents.

00:07:20.173 --> 00:07:23.376
This PDF page contains diagrams, equations,

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.612
and a lot of text. You will also use Pillow

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:29.515
to load the image and check its dimensions.

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:34.821
This high resolution screenshot preserves all visual details from the original PDF.

00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:38.458
ColPali processes images by dividing them into a grid of patches,

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:41.360
similar to how a Vision Transformer works.

00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:44.764
For ColPali v1.3,

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:47.667
this is always a fixed 32 by 32 grid.

00:07:48.067 --> 00:07:52.672
For ColSmol, the grid dimensions adapt to the image's aspect ratio.

00:07:53.072 --> 00:07:57.677
Let's visualize how this particular image is divided into patches

00:07:57.677 --> 00:08:00.379
by adding some spacing between them.

00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:03.316
You have a helper method to do exactly this.

00:08:03.816 --> 00:08:07.019
Now, you will process the image through ColPali's processor.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.323
This processor converts the image into a batch of tensors

00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:14.527
containing input IDs, attention masks, and pixel values

00:08:14.727 --> 00:08:16.729
that the model can understand.

00:08:17.029 --> 00:08:19.832
The processed batch contains input IDs

00:08:20.032 --> 00:08:24.036
that vary by model. It's a 1031 for ColPali

00:08:24.537 --> 00:08:26.539
or a 1139 for ColSmol.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:29.842
Let's decode this to see how ColPali

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:31.544
represents the image internally.

00:08:32.879 --> 00:08:34.881
Notice the sequence contains

00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:37.984
1024 special image tokens

00:08:38.284 --> 00:08:40.486
representing different patches of the image,

00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:42.388
followed by instruction tokens.

00:08:42.622 --> 00:08:45.424
ColPali processes the image as a grid of patches,

00:08:45.525 --> 00:08:47.527
creating embeddings for each patch.

00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:51.531
For ColPali, it's always a fixed 32x32 grid,

00:08:51.631 --> 00:08:55.234
while for ColSmol, the grid dimensions vary based on image size.

00:08:55.334 --> 00:08:57.537
Let's generate embeddings for the image.

00:08:57.737 --> 00:09:00.740
You end up with 128 dimensional vector

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.209
for each token you just saw.

00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:06.212
For searching instead, we may prefer the embeddings

00:09:06.212 --> 00:09:09.215
corresponding to the image tokens, not the instruction tokens.

00:09:09.682 --> 00:09:12.485
using a helper function to extract the image mask,

00:09:12.485 --> 00:09:16.188
we isolate the 1024 image patch embeddings.

00:09:16.188 --> 00:09:18.491
This number is fixed for ColPali

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:20.893
and might be different for ColSmol, but

00:09:20.893 --> 00:09:24.497
by a random chance it's exactly the same number of patches created.

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:28.200
One powerful feature of ColPali is its interpretability.

00:09:28.801 --> 00:09:31.404
We can visualize exactly which parts of an image

00:09:31.404 --> 00:09:34.407
are most relevant to specific query tokens.

00:09:34.707 --> 00:09:39.111
Unlike traditional dense embeddings that produce a single opaque similarity score,

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:42.114
ColPali's token-level embeddings allow us to see

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:44.517
how each word in our query relates

00:09:44.517 --> 00:09:46.218
to different regions of an image.

00:09:46.485 --> 00:09:49.288
This makes the model's reasoning transparent

00:09:49.288 --> 00:09:52.592
and helps us understand why certain documents are retrieved.

00:09:53.092 --> 00:09:56.295
Let's explore this capability by creating a query

00:09:56.295 --> 00:09:59.899
and visualizing how it matches different parts of our document.

00:10:00.499 --> 00:10:03.402
We'll create a query asking about the transformer architecture.

00:10:04.003 --> 00:10:06.505
This page from the Attention Is All You Need paper

00:10:06.505 --> 00:10:08.507
contains the famous transformer diagram,

00:10:08.908 --> 00:10:12.311
so we expect ColPali to highlight those specific regions

00:10:12.311 --> 00:10:14.814
when processing our query. You will process the

00:10:14.814 --> 00:10:17.350
query through the model to get query embeddings.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:22.254
Each query token is also represented by a 128 dimensional vector.

00:10:22.555 --> 00:10:26.125
But there are just 21 tokens created out of this query.

00:10:26.525 --> 00:10:29.395
Let's clean it up a little bit by removing padding

00:10:29.495 --> 00:10:32.798
and augmentation tokens, and then retokenize,

00:10:33.099 --> 00:10:37.703
so we can see the individual query tokens to analyze them further.

00:10:37.703 --> 00:10:40.906
To create our similarity visualizations, we first need to calculate

00:10:40.906 --> 00:10:44.310
how many patches the image was divided into.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:47.613
You will also extract the image mask to isolate

00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:49.215
just the image token embeddings,

00:10:49.215 --> 00:10:51.217
excluding the instruction tokens.

00:10:51.517 --> 00:10:54.120
Now you will generate the similarity maps

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.522
using ColPali's interpretability tools.

00:10:56.722 --> 00:10:59.925
This computes the similarity between each query token

00:11:00.026 --> 00:11:01.727
and each image patch,

00:11:01.727 --> 00:11:05.131
creating a heat map for every token in our query.

00:11:05.464 --> 00:11:08.768
Let's visualize the similarity map for a specific query token.

00:11:08.768 --> 00:11:11.671
You will select the layer token from the query

00:11:11.671 --> 00:11:15.941
to see which parts of the image it attends to most strongly.

00:11:16.342 --> 00:11:19.545
This visualization will overlay a heat map

00:11:19.545 --> 00:11:22.214
on the original image with warmer colors

00:11:22.214 --> 00:11:25.217
indicating regions that ColPali considers

00:11:25.217 --> 00:11:27.720
more relevant to this specific word.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:31.123
This interpretability feature is valuable

00:11:31.123 --> 00:11:33.826
for debugging and understanding your actual system.

00:11:34.126 --> 00:11:38.230
You can identify whether the model is focusing on the right content,

00:11:38.531 --> 00:11:40.533
discover unexpected matching patterns,

00:11:40.733 --> 00:11:43.135
and build trust in the system's decisions.

00:11:43.335 --> 00:11:46.238
In production applications, this transparency

00:11:46.238 --> 00:11:48.941
can also help explain retrieval results to its users.

00:11:49.642 --> 00:11:52.645
However, since we are using the smaller ColSmol model here,

00:11:52.645 --> 00:11:55.848
you can see that the model seems to be paying attention

00:11:55.848 --> 00:11:59.151
to some background patches, which is slightly unexpected.

00:11:59.452 --> 00:12:02.254
Let's see how it looks like for the full ColPali model.

00:12:03.055 --> 00:12:04.990
This is how the same similarity map would

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:07.226
look like for the bigger ColPali model.

00:12:07.526 --> 00:12:09.628
ColSmol performs some pixel shuffling,

00:12:09.729 --> 00:12:12.431
so the model was focusing on some random locations.

00:12:12.631 --> 00:12:14.400
But for ColPali, you can see the

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:16.435
model mostly pays attention to the word layer

00:12:16.936 --> 00:12:19.538
occurring multiple times on that page, which is expected,

00:12:19.839 --> 00:12:22.241
as that's the token you were visualizing.

00:12:22.775 --> 00:12:26.245
Let's build a practical multimodal search system using Qdrant.

00:12:26.912 --> 00:12:29.582
We'll set up a collection with multi-vector support,

00:12:29.882 --> 00:12:34.553
using MaxSim comparator to handle ColPali's multiple embeddings per document.

00:12:34.854 --> 00:12:37.556
You will also disable HNSW graph creation

00:12:37.656 --> 00:12:40.259
as Qdrant can't use it effectively either way.

00:12:40.559 --> 00:12:42.361
Now, you will index all pages from

00:12:42.361 --> 00:12:44.163
the Attention is All You Need paper.

00:12:45.131 --> 00:12:48.467
The load precomputed flag is set to true

00:12:48.467 --> 00:12:51.604
which will load precomputed vector data for each page,

00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:53.506
rather than computing it live.

00:12:53.506 --> 00:12:57.276
Each page’s vectors are uploaded to Qdrant

00:12:57.276 --> 00:13:00.846
along with the image’s file path as metadata.

00:13:01.113 --> 00:13:03.215
Let's create a helper search function

00:13:03.215 --> 00:13:07.720
that processes text queries and create ColPali embeddings for them.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:12.324
The same function will also query the collection you've just created

00:13:12.324 --> 00:13:14.326
to get the closest matches.

00:13:14.493 --> 00:13:16.796
Since we are working with visual data,

00:13:16.796 --> 00:13:18.697
we have another helper method

00:13:18.697 --> 00:13:22.301
that displays the search results on the screen.

00:13:22.301 --> 00:13:24.503
Let's search for model architecture to see

00:13:24.503 --> 00:13:26.505
which pages of the paper

00:13:26.505 --> 00:13:29.708
the model considers to be the best hits.

00:13:29.708 --> 00:13:31.410
We ask for the model architecture.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:34.213
So it's not surprising that the

00:13:34.213 --> 00:13:37.016
most famous diagram is coming up first.

00:13:37.216 --> 00:13:40.219
But there is also a certain section of the paper

00:13:40.219 --> 00:13:42.221
which is called Model Architecture.

00:13:42.621 --> 00:13:45.224
So it's also selected as one of the best

00:13:45.424 --> 00:13:47.927
pages to answer that particular question.

00:13:48.327 --> 00:13:52.832
Next, let's search for scaled dot-product attention.

00:13:53.332 --> 00:13:57.536
Again, the diagrams in the first result returned

00:13:57.536 --> 00:13:59.672
are presenting exactly this, but there

00:13:59.672 --> 00:14:02.141
is also a part of the document

00:14:02.141 --> 00:14:04.543
which is called scaled dot-product attention

00:14:04.543 --> 00:14:07.346
and it also got captured by the ColPali retrieval.

00:14:07.847 --> 00:14:10.249
Finally, let's search for experiment results.

00:14:11.016 --> 00:14:13.886
The goal here is to retrieve pages containing tables and performance metrics

00:14:13.886 --> 00:14:16.755
that we would expect the paper to contain.

00:14:17.423 --> 00:14:20.025
ColPali seems to understand both textual content

00:14:20.025 --> 00:14:22.628
and visual elements like tables and charts.

00:14:23.128 --> 00:14:25.030
Surprisingly, the first page of the whole

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:26.932
paper is returned as a top match,

00:14:27.533 --> 00:14:29.935
even though it doesn't seem to contain

00:14:29.935 --> 00:14:32.538
any kind of a table summarizing some experiments.

00:14:32.638 --> 00:14:36.308
Nevertheless, the experiment results could have been mentioned in the abstract

00:14:36.342 --> 00:14:38.277
or maybe in the footnotes of the

00:14:38.277 --> 00:14:40.846
article, so it's nothing that's surprising.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:43.482
Nevertheless, the second and the third match,

00:14:43.782 --> 00:14:46.485
they both contain some tables summarizing the efficiency

00:14:46.785 --> 00:14:48.988
of that particular method versus the other methods.

00:14:49.488 --> 00:14:51.156
And this is actually something we would

00:14:51.156 --> 00:14:53.092
expect from this kind of system to return.

00:14:53.425 --> 00:14:57.263
To sum it up, ColPali enables powerful multimodal search

00:14:57.263 --> 00:15:01.667
over visual documents without requiring an OCR step

00:15:01.667 --> 00:15:03.936
or complex document processing.

00:15:04.336 --> 00:15:08.140
by processing documents directly as images,

00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:12.244
it captures both textual and visual semantics naturally.

00:15:12.945 --> 00:15:15.915
Multi-vector representations come with certain limitations.

00:15:16.315 --> 00:15:19.018
Assuming each of the documents needs half a megabyte

00:15:19.018 --> 00:15:20.619
and you have a million documents,

00:15:20.953 --> 00:15:24.356
You may end up with 500 gigabytes of memory needed

00:15:24.356 --> 00:15:26.759
just to keep all the ColPali embeddings for search.

00:15:27.159 --> 00:15:29.261
That might be fine for low-scale projects

00:15:29.261 --> 00:15:31.764
with thousands of documents, but in practice,

00:15:31.764 --> 00:15:33.866
you often deal with billions of them

00:15:33.866 --> 00:15:36.969
and it would require a significant amount of memory to run.

00:15:37.136 --> 00:15:40.339
Having a million documents in a retrieval system is quite likely

00:15:40.539 --> 00:15:42.942
if your company has a lot of data to search over.

00:15:43.542 --> 00:15:47.146
Typically, these vectors will contain a lot of redundant information,

00:15:47.246 --> 00:15:51.350
and some interesting optimization techniques can help you reduce the RAM consumption.

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:53.919
Let's discuss these methods in the next lesson.
